This license covers everything in this repository (together, "the Work"): the
raw planning prompt, the Claude Code and OpenAI Codex skill packages, the plan
template, references and addenda, example plans, the Python validators, the
website source, and the documentation.

Plain-English summary (the numbered terms below are what counts): free for
individuals, paid and by permission for companies, teams, and client work,
attribution always, no warranty.

1. FREE PERMITTED USE - no permission needed

   The following uses are free, forever, with no sign-up and no payment:

   a. Personal use by an individual: personal projects, learning,
      experimentation, and side projects.
   b. Individual workplace use: one employed person using the Work at their
      job, by their own individual choice, for their own tasks only.
      Generating plans or other output for other people at the organization,
      as a service to the organization, or that the organization relies on
      beyond that individual's own work, is organizational use under
      section 2(a) - one person may not operate the Work on behalf of an
      unlicensed organization. This permission ends when the conditions in
      section 2(a) are met.
   c. A freelancer's own personal projects (not client deliverables).
   d. Reading and studying the Work, by anyone, including employees of any
      organization.

   Individuals may privately adapt the Work for their own permitted use.
   Private adaptations must not be shared, published, or distributed.

2. COMMERCIAL LICENSE REQUIRED - ask first, then pay

   A paid commercial license, agreed in advance with the copyright holder,
   is required for:

   a. Organizational use: use of the Work, in whole or in part, by an
      organization - meaning any of the following, whichever happens first:
      (i) two or more people at the same organization use it; (ii) it is
      set, recommended, or documented as a team or company standard; or
      (iii) it is embedded into the organization's tooling, onboarding
      material, internal documentation, or playbooks.
   b. Client work: any use of the Work where the output serves or is
      delivered to a paying third party - including use by freelancers,
      agencies, and consultancies on client projects. This applies
      regardless of business form: performing client work as a sole trader,
      through a single-person company, or through any other vehicle does
      not turn client work into personal use.

   "Organization" means a company, firm, charity, public body, or other
   entity, together with its parents, subsidiaries, and affiliates, and
   includes contractors and agency workers engaged by any of them. People
   working for or within any of these count as being at the same
   organization; splitting use across group entities or contractor
   arrangements does not avoid section 2(a).

   To obtain a commercial license, contact hello@Veedence.co.uk before use.

3. NO EXTRACTION

   No portion of the Work may be copied, adapted, or incorporated into any
   other product, prompt collection, internal standard, training material,
   tool, or published work without prior written permission from the
   copyright holder (normally as part of a commercial license). This applies
   regardless of how small the portion is. Section 1's private-adaptation
   allowance does not permit sharing or embedding adapted material anywhere
   beyond the individual's own use.

4. ATTRIBUTION - required, not optional

   The required attribution line is, verbatim:

       Robust Feature Planner by Simeon Williams - Veedence.co.uk

   It must appear:

   a. In every copy or fork of the Work: this LICENSE file and the
      attribution in the README must remain intact and unmodified.
   b. In any adaptation made under a granted license: the line above plus
      "Adapted from the original with permission," at the top of the adapted
      material or in its header.
   c. In any plan or document generated with the Work that is shared with
      anyone other than the person who generated it: the attribution line in
      the document's Document Control section or footer must not be removed.

